==North Frisian==

===Etymology===
The reason for the strange three-way spelling of {{IPAchar|[f]}} in Sylt Frisian (explained below) is that it reflects a situation that existed in the early 20th century when the system was devised: Those cases spelt ⟨{{l|frr||f}}⟩ had long been voiceless, those spelt ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ were then still reliably voiced, while those spelt ⟨{{l|frr||v}}⟩ were half-voiced or variable.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|frr|[v]|aa=generally}}
* {{IPA|frr|[f]|aa=[[Sylt]], except at the start of a word or stressed syllable}}

===Letter===
{{letter|frr}}

# {{Latn-def|frr|letter}}

====Usage notes====
* Word-internal and final ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ alternates with ⟨{{l|frr|f}}⟩ in many cases. In Föhr-Amrum and Mooring Frisian this alternation simply reflects the pronunciation: voiced {{IPAchar|[v]}} and devoiced {{IPAchar|[f]}} respectively. It is true that written ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ might be devoiced in certain clusters, e.g. {{m|frr|dü [[skriiw|skree'''w'''st]]}}, but careful enunciation maintains {{IPAchar|[v]}} in these cases, so the spelling ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ is justified. On Amrum (but not on Föhr), final unstressed ⟨{{l|frr||ew}}⟩ is pronounced {{IPAchar|[o]}}.
* Sylt Frisian has lost the voice distinction in unstressed position. Therefore ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ frequently represents {{IPAchar|[f]}} in this dialect, which moreover replaces ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ with ⟨{{l|frr|v}}⟩ under certain circumstances. The result is that all of ⟨{{l|frr||w, v, f}}⟩ may alternate in one word stem without there being any difference in pronunciation. Some stems are non-alternating; they keep ⟨{{l|frr||f}}⟩ throughout (see e.g. {{m|frr|Wüf}}, {{m|frr|Filosoof}}). However, most are alternating; they use ⟨{{l|frr||w}}⟩ before vowels, ⟨{{l|frr||v}}⟩ word-finally after long or unstressed vowels, ⟨{{l|frr||f}}⟩ word-finally after short stressed vowels and generally in consonant clusters (compare the conjugation of {{m|frr|skriiv}}).

