===Etymology===
The lowercase form of {{m|nb|W}}. The letter developed in medieval Latin-script writing from a doubled form of the letter represented in modern typography by ⟨u⟩ or ⟨v⟩, which were not yet consistently distinguished as separate letters. The doubled form was used to represent the Germanic sound {{IPAchar|/w/}}, for which Classical Latin had no separate letter, and gradually developed into the independent letter ⟨w⟩. Its Norwegian name, {{m|nb|dobbelt-v|t=double v}}, refers to its shape and historical origin.

